 "chunks": [
  {
   "text": "How can anyone trust seventeen million lines no human has read?",
   "start": 0.725,
   "end": 4.562
  },
  {
   "text": "In Lean, every step must follow from earlier ones by fixed rules,",
   "start": 4.562,
   "end": 8.875
  },
  {
   "text": "and a small program, the kernel, checks them all.",
   "start": 8.875,
   "end": 12.237
  },
  {
   "text": "Kevin Buzzard lists four questions to ask of AI-written Lean.",
   "start": 12.237,
   "end": 15.975
  },
  {
   "text": "Is the statement right?",
   "start": 16.875,
   "end": 18.212
  },
  {
   "text": "Here it is Mathlib's own definition of the Riemann Hypothesis,",
   "start": 18.212,
   "end": 21.488
  },
  {
   "text": "vetted by experts.",
   "start": 21.488,
   "end": 23.05
  },
  {
   "text": "Does it compile?",
   "start": 23.05,
   "end": 24.163
  },
  {
   "text": "Dozens of people have rebuilt it.",
   "start": 24.163,
   "end": 26.05
  },
  {
   "text": "Does it prove exactly that statement, using only the standard axioms?",
   "start": 26.05,
   "end": 30.125
  },
  {
   "text": "Two independent tools say yes.",
   "start": 30.125,
   "end": 32.425
  },
  {
   "text": "Could it exploit a bug in Lean itself?",
   "start": 32.425,
   "end": 34.75
  },
  {
   "text": "Several independent checkers accepted it,",
   "start": 34.75,
   "end": 37.087
  },
  {
   "text": "two of them formally proved unable to accept a false proof.",
   "start": 37.087,
   "end": 40.7
  },
  {
   "text": "So on November twenty-first, the Lean and Mathlib communities wrote:",
   "start": 41.625,
   "end": 45.925
  },
  {
   "text": "we have full confidence that Alpöge's proof of the Riemann Hypothesis is correct.",
   "start": 45.925,
   "end": 50.625
  }
 ],
